#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221098 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "11b5421d-e98b-5e37-9bdd-bec13f010118" :question/math-academy-id "e-21503" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\}  = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$  find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{0}^{t} \\cos\\big(\\pi\\tau\\big) \\, \\textrm{d}\\tau, \\quad s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t) = \\cos(\\pi t).$ Thus, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221099 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "58ed1920-cd4f-5993-b3bf-1fa4e43492af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21527" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221104 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51dfecdb-ad66-5e07-8873-ca8e116b27bd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Formula for Laplace Transforms of Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "602b037e-28bf-5afd-a466-652668bf08c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21551" :question/problem "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "692043f4-7e66-506a-ba5e-49fcbb403441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379293} {:answer/id #uuid "2bea080b-d507-5198-a78e-e08d7fd92ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379294} {:answer/id #uuid "69a354d5-45fa-54a4-bb04-9a17b49a739d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379295} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbefa14-9f99-5313-bb03-ead214d140f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379296} {:answer/id #uuid "b9175d08-909c-5c51-8ea2-6de9bea4e802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbefa14-9f99-5313-bb03-ead214d140f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379296} :answer-field/id #uuid "96da93bb-f22c-5fc6-bf98-b209ef94ad3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f8057e6-82f2-5886-becf-1eb97b378527" :question/math-academy-id "q-327935" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{L}^{-1}{\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a83e2d2-6ad2-5163-9953-40c98334a6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379300} {:answer/id #uuid "1179d763-9a04-5b0e-ab1f-1aed32a7f80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379301} {:answer/id #uuid "a39393a5-8aec-542d-80f4-8aef6a099765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379302} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf0ac34-4b2e-508c-aafc-04ab91865ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{s-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379303} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c12643-6761-57c0-92c3-04eb96d6fccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a83e2d2-6ad2-5163-9953-40c98334a6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379300} :answer-field/id #uuid "752a4868-8082-57b8-b153-fc1e5301ba55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "771a6f1c-1290-511e-b128-638bd156cabe" :question/math-academy-id "q-330825" :question/problem "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{4τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Thus, for $s>4,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>4,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{4τ}\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "318b1fc5-c8ac-5222-97c4-516823c1b3e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379307} {:answer/id #uuid "480c8123-37fa-5b01-9148-d3afac4a6f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379308} {:answer/id #uuid "ebeeeade-5a9a-50d6-8b3f-06bdeba2eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379309} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a4f89e-487b-5285-81d0-8b9dc352381c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{s+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379310} {:answer/id #uuid "99a5d590-73a1-5fee-8891-d145e859422b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebeeeade-5a9a-50d6-8b3f-06bdeba2eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379309} :answer-field/id #uuid "274f7524-cbad-52f6-b50b-80576d79abf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5a6c667-8ec3-50a3-8a5a-a2ecda7f0e19" :question/math-academy-id "q-330826" :question/problem "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>-3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{-3t}.$ Thus, for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e20a848b-a44a-5c84-97e7-21e2721f2026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379314} {:answer/id #uuid "573f3873-07bc-5a5c-bb26-97b4144bbd46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379315} {:answer/id #uuid "8725e906-60bf-5f77-99cb-14ecf73e8f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379316} {:answer/id #uuid "88f0e944-9425-569e-8dc1-d22f998f1d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379317} {:answer/id #uuid "cce9f4fe-4f9c-5c5a-9d6e-7fc44ff895c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e20a848b-a44a-5c84-97e7-21e2721f2026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379314} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3244e0-0525-550e-a255-7f80602a47b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84ac39af-5a4f-5a7b-bd9f-24d50174d342" :question/math-academy-id "q-330831" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{3}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d55888b8-e3f1-5f48-a556-6ce46e7bc95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379321} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5b40c3-6bb8-53c0-ba65-b650e8c3b4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379322} {:answer/id #uuid "1c126a11-c24e-5907-a06e-39c9e3bf62da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379323} {:answer/id #uuid "c7658c56-01de-5952-87d3-95530f751fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379324} {:answer/id #uuid "6042d970-55f4-547c-9fab-c02aca569b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6042d970-55f4-547c-9fab-c02aca569b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379325} :answer-field/id #uuid "31db30ac-c995-5373-ac95-912b2869a939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe76a17-debc-55e3-a3e3-b3e4019267d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-330834" :question/problem "Given that $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3τ)\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s({s}^{2}+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b47ce0-32ab-5b50-9b8b-ee4861c62196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379328} {:answer/id #uuid "abce33e4-2d96-5464-a5b0-6908a3e845cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379329} {:answer/id #uuid "f661c2b1-3acd-5afe-b3a1-7859d5c014a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379330} {:answer/id #uuid "6609b22c-025c-57a5-a9b1-94506068986d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379331} {:answer/id #uuid "f78d80df-94d8-5cac-9fff-be341f7fd8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b47ce0-32ab-5b50-9b8b-ee4861c62196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379328} :answer-field/id #uuid "22356902-cfe6-504c-857d-359926da79cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "899d127d-9644-5e01-a5c9-97f7434aab15" :question/math-academy-id "q-330835" :question/problem "Given that $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3499ec3c-af21-540f-82dd-3b29b6b87b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379335} {:answer/id #uuid "dba0d752-e2ae-5815-815a-ddea162f3cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379336} {:answer/id #uuid "20c5d8f1-315e-5f98-81d7-cd5dcc8d6a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379337} {:answer/id #uuid "140946c5-a2e6-561e-8a5a-cea3612338e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379338} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a7af40-380e-5325-b128-5f2b3a787bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "140946c5-a2e6-561e-8a5a-cea3612338e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379338} :answer-field/id #uuid "76a7ef8a-6e41-5ecb-8e1c-900979b600fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60e4c590-2cee-5f38-a80e-2339267d9a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-330838" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a7f4e7e-7357-50a0-9909-30b738690cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379342} {:answer/id #uuid "df4aa97d-76a5-5529-a9b4-c3c6623d09fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{6}}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379343} {:answer/id #uuid "ade2c17e-1ff2-5ab5-937f-d45c8a27fc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{t}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379344} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4af458-8e8f-5ded-88cc-5e78f74c4079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379345} {:answer/id #uuid "089b5ce5-70f8-5899-aff6-f007db63b4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a7f4e7e-7357-50a0-9909-30b738690cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379342} :answer-field/id #uuid "b228cf5f-18a3-567c-b410-b04cf395b6fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01465358-c6b8-5ae6-b779-bb5cbb3136fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-330843" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{24}{{s}^{6}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{24}{{s}^{6}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{4!}{{s}^{4+1}}} \\\\  & ={t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{4}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74f8333e-c013-5004-a153-a044d6e5f54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{8}}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379349} {:answer/id #uuid "dafe35c1-d10d-538b-a04a-99d6625f0305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379350} {:answer/id #uuid "950c95cd-a26c-58c0-b4a0-e6645f55d248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379351} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbf830f-a49b-51ee-bbc8-8615c161cc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379352} {:answer/id #uuid "c3642f6c-90d4-5f1a-a4e8-de424396c6a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{720}{{t}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafe35c1-d10d-538b-a04a-99d6625f0305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379350} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ae8a8f-c489-53da-b83b-3cb9de6dd434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5767dcb-98c0-56bf-97f2-b10c194c3088" :question/math-academy-id "q-330845" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{6!}{{s}^{6+1}}} \\\\  & ={t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{6}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422c77bf-3a47-5faf-a77d-bff8d9ba09de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379356} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4289e9-674a-54e1-8c7d-1b04fc174125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{5t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379357} {:answer/id #uuid "e72ac0cb-6b68-557f-a079-b87d763aebe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379358} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bbe226-a477-56ee-b670-3797aad8af5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(1-{e}^{5t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379359} {:answer/id #uuid "34039386-a29e-5f07-8dd9-e3415a6444e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c4289e9-674a-54e1-8c7d-1b04fc174125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{5t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379357} :answer-field/id #uuid "66091e8f-e117-501d-bd76-24a4acfb2ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41def00c-216c-5d5d-9bd6-937f20d64110" :question/math-academy-id "q-330849" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-5)},\\,s>5\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-5)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-5}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-5}} \\\\  & ={e}^{5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{5τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{5t}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51dfecdb-ad66-5e07-8873-ca8e116b27bd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Formula for Laplace Transforms of Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98e34cc-5bdb-5e57-acd9-dbd3997a6c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(τ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220182} {:answer/id #uuid "12084a89-8e50-55e6-9cc7-2e5027bec3d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220183} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad37fd8-a163-51d2-87c2-89d720b65d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(τ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220184} {:answer/id #uuid "7529c7b2-5aa7-54ab-80a0-c428e0f14e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(τ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad37fd8-a163-51d2-87c2-89d720b65d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(τ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220184} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a14b47-579f-5747-b81d-d32ee18e01bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e18775e9-dd92-56fe-baf9-cc96f8ca69b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379362} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b55cc1-959f-56cd-9f22-a778834b222c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379363} {:answer/id #uuid "26013260-50a2-57bc-8e6f-018f5dd03a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379364} {:answer/id #uuid "d21ce9d7-ec9b-545f-a1b3-52db16ed58a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/va